<title>RUSSIA: Seven CIS members have industry growth in Jan-Sept.</title>
<headline>Seven CIS members have industry growth in Jan-Sept.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1996-11-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Industrial production grew in only seven members of the Commonwealth of Independent States in the first nine months of this year, the CIS Interstate Statistics Committee said.</p>
<p>Average output in the group of 12 former Soviet republics was 3.3 percent lower than a year earlier, after a 6.4 percent fall in the same 1995 period, it said in its latest bulletin.</p>
<p>The biggest increases in output were recorded in the central Asian states of Turkmenistan and Kyrgyzstan, both recovering from big falls a year earlier.</p>
<p>The CIS comprises all former republics of the Soviet Union, except the Baltic states.</p>
<p>The committee issued the following data (percentage change on year-ago period, except for consumer price inflation, which is percentage change on previous month):</p>
<p>			 GDP	 Industrial  production    Inflation</p>
<p>		   Jan-Sept 96   Jan-Sept 96  Jan-Sept 95    Sept 96</p>
<p> Armenia	    +4.5	    +1.5	    +2.3	    -1.1</p>
<p> Azerbaijan	 +0.7	    -8.0	   -25.6	    +0.8</p>
<p> Belarus	    +1		+2.6	   -11.0	    +1.8</p>
<p> Georgia	   +12		+5.2	   -18.8	    +0.5</p>
<p> Kazakhstan	 +1.1	    +0.1	   -10.6	    +1.2</p>
<p> Kyrgyzstan	 +3	     +11.2	   -14.5	    +0.5</p>
<p> Moldova	    -4.6	    -3.0	   -12.1	    +1.5</p>
<p> Russia	     -6		-5		-3		+0.3</p>
<p> Tajikistan	-18	     -20.2	    -9.5	    -2.9</p>
<p> Turkmenistan	n/a	   +25.9	   -18.1	     n/a</p>
<p> Ukraine	   -10		-4.3	   -14.4	    +2.0</p>
<p> Uzbekistan	 +1.5	    +5.7	   -12.0	     n/a</p>
<p>NOTE - Moldova GDP is first six months.</p>
<p>--Jonathan Lynn, Moscow Newsroom, +7095 941 8520</p>
